    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d754">

                <pb facs="1028" n="1014" sameAs="frus:frus1944v06/pg1014"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">711.932/17</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The Ambassador in China</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic"
                        >Gauss</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">to the Secretary of State </hi>
                </head>
                <opener>
                    <seg rendition="#float-left">No. 2431</seg>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">Chungking</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1944-04-13">April 13,
                        1944</date>.<lb/>[Received May 3.]</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>
                    <hi rendition="#small-caps">Sir</hi>: With reference to my despatch no. 2411 of
                    April 8th, on the subject of the right of aliens to acquire and hold land in
                    China, I have now the honor to enclose copy of a memorandum of conversation <pb
                        facs="1029" n="1015" xml:id="pg1015"/> with the Minister for Foreign Affairs
                    together with copy of a memorandum handed me by the latter outlining the treaty
                    provisions and provisions of Chinese law on foreign landholding in China.<anchor
                        n="16" xml:id="d754-f1"/>
                </p>
                <p>The enclosures contribute nothing further to the general subject as discussed in
                    despatch no. 2411, but it will be of interest to note the remark to me by the
                    Foreign Minister that China is disposed to grant to Americans the same rights
                    granted to other foreign nationals and that China will be prepared to concede to
                    Americans the right to acquire and hold real property in China.</p>
                <p>Respectfully yours,</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>C. E. <hi rendition="#small-caps">Gauss</hi>
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
